<!-- §01 — The Missed Moment -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Missed Moment</div>
<h2>The AI That Only Wakes Up When You Ask</h2>
<p>A person collapses on a security camera feed. A dart hits the board while you count throws. A fire starts in the corner of the frame. These moments share one property: they do not wait for you to ask about them. The instant passes, and it is gone.</p>
<p>Today's large models are built around the opposite assumption. They are <strong>turn-based by design</strong> — they open their eyes only when you speak, answer only when addressed, and otherwise remain completely inert. This is not a matter of speed. Even the fastest turn-based model has no mechanism to react to an event that arrives with no accompanying user prompt. By construction, it cannot.</p>
<p>Consumer video-call apps have tried to paper over this with polling: Doubao fires an external trigger every few seconds; Gemini does not even do that, answering only about the single frame visible when you ask. But polling replaces one structural problem with another — the model can never react faster than the polling interval, and the timing decision lives in an external clock, not in the model that understands the scene.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">turn-based vs. interaction model — timing on a live event</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Trigger Event to simulate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="timing-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.625rem;margin-top:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button id="timing-trigger" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.75rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--amber);transition:all .15s">🔥 Trigger Event</button>
      <button id="timing-reset" style="padding:.3rem .9rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.75rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);transition:all .15s">↺ Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>JoyAI-VL-Interaction breaks with this paradigm by making the decision of <em>when to respond</em> a native, learned capability of the model itself — chosen every second, independent of any user prompt. In the fall detection case, it raises the alert at the instant of the fall. Doubao arrives four to five seconds later. Gemini admits it cannot monitor the scene at all.</p>
<p>The result is not a faster turn-based model. It is a different kind of model entirely: one that is <em>present</em> in the world the way a person is, continuously watching what is happening and deciding on its own whether the moment calls for a word.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 — Three Actions, Every Second -->
<section id="s-actions" data-code="actions">
<div class="section-no">02 — Three Actions, Every Second</div>
<h2>Speak, Silence, or Delegate</h2>
<p>At the heart of JoyAI-VL-Interaction is a simple and radical idea: at every second of a live video stream, the model takes exactly one of three actions.</p>
<ul>
  <li><span class="action-chip speak">◆ speak</span> — emit a <code>&lt;/response&gt;</code> token and reply to the user</li>
  <li><span class="action-chip silence">— silence</span> — emit a <code>&lt;/silence&gt;</code> token and keep watching</li>
  <li><span class="action-chip delegate">⟳ delegate</span> — hand a hard problem to a background model asynchronously</li>
</ul>
<p>Silence is not the absence of a decision — it is a first-class action, labeled explicitly in training data. The model must learn to <em>wait</em> rather than filling every second with speech. In any real stream, most seconds should be silent; getting this right is what makes the model feel present rather than noisy.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">per-second action timeline — dart throw counting scenario</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click seconds to see the model's decision</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="timeline-canvas" style="width:100%;height:140px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div id="timeline-info" style="margin-top:.75rem;min-height:36px;font-size:.8rem;color:var(--muted2);font-family:var(--ff-mono)"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The three timing types in training data</h3>
<p>To teach timing — not just content — the training pipeline constructs three distinct timing types around every event:</p>
<div class="step-list" id="timing-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Backward — answer arrives after the question</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Question asked at t=7s about a dart that landed at t=5s. Model sees the evidence and responds immediately at t=7s when the question arrives.</div>
      <div class="step-code">t=5: [dart lands] → silence | t=7: [user asks] → respond</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Present — answer arrives exactly as the event happens</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Question asked at t=5s, and the dart lands at t=5s. Model responds the moment the evidence appears.</div>
      <div class="step-code">t=5: [dart + question simultaneous] → respond immediately</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Forward — model stays silent until the event happens</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Question asked at t=3s about a dart throw that hasn't happened yet. Model stays silent at t=3 and t=4, then responds the instant the dart lands at t=5.</div>
      <div class="step-code">t=3: [question] → silence | t=4 → silence | t=5: [dart] → respond</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
