<!-- §03 — AdaCodec -->
<section id="s-codec" data-code="codec">
<div class="section-no">03 — AdaCodec</div>
<h2>Watching Without Burning the Budget</h2>
<p>Continuously watching an unbounded video stream is expensive. A naive approach re-encodes every frame through a full ViT, spending ~256 tokens per frame regardless of how much has changed. At 1 frame per second, a two-hour stream would require over 7,000 full ViT passes — far beyond any practical context window.</p>
<p>JoyAI-VL-Interaction uses <strong>AdaCodec</strong>, a predictive visual codec inspired by video compression. The key idea: transmit only what prediction cannot explain.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Reference frames</strong> — full ViT encoding (~256 tokens) whenever the scene changes significantly</li>
  <li><strong>P-frames</strong> — compact residual + motion tokens (~16 tokens) for predictable in-between frames</li>
  <li>A <strong>predictive-cost reset</strong> triggers a new reference frame whenever the prediction becomes expensive</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">AdaCodec token usage — 12-frame stream with one scene change</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag slider to see token savings</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="codec-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;margin-top:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <label style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2)">Scene change at frame:</label>
      <input type="range" id="codec-slider" min="2" max="10" value="5" style="flex:1;accent-color:var(--purple)">
      <span id="codec-label" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--cyan);min-width:60px"></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <p>For a 2-hour stream with one scene change per minute, AdaCodec uses approximately <strong>120 × 256 + (7,080 × 16) = ~144k tokens</strong> vs. naive frame-by-frame encoding's <strong>7,200 × 256 = ~1.85M tokens</strong> — a <strong>13× reduction</strong> in context budget.</p>
</div>

<p>This is not an approximation trick — it encodes only what matters. When the scene is static (a stable security feed, a talking head on a call), P-frames capture it in 16 tokens. When it changes dramatically (a fire ignites, a person enters), a new reference frame fires immediately at full quality. The model reads an interleaved stream of reference tokens and P-tokens, and the context budget grows with how much the scene <em>changes</em> rather than with frame count.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 — Teaching Timing -->
<section id="s-training" data-code="training">
<div class="section-no">04 — Teaching Timing</div>
<h2>4M Clips and a Weighted Loss</h2>
<p>Building a model that watches the world requires a fundamentally different kind of training data. Standard video QA data pairs questions with answers — it teaches <em>what to say</em>, but says nothing about <em>when to say it</em>. JoyAI-VL-Interaction is trained on <strong>time-aligned data</strong>: over 4 million streaming clips where every single second is labeled with the correct action (speak, silence, or delegate).</p>

<p>The six data families cover the full range of real-world streaming scenarios:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Proactive alerting and anomaly detection on live feeds</li>
  <li>Time-aligned question answering (backward / present / forward timing)</li>
  <li>Real-time counting and perception over time</li>
  <li>Live commentary and narration</li>
  <li>Multi-turn casual chat in varied styles</li>
  <li>Delegation episodes pairing hard off-stream subtasks with on-stream choreography</li>
</ul>

<h3>The imbalance problem — and how the weighted loss solves it</h3>
<p>In any real stream, silence vastly outnumbers speech. Under a standard cross-entropy loss, this imbalance pushes gradients toward "always stay silent" and drowns out the signal for when to actually respond. JoyAI-VL-Interaction uses a <strong>role-weighted loss</strong>:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Weighted Cross-Entropy Loss</div>
  <span class="math-var">L</span>(θ) = −<span style="font-size:.95em">1/|A|</span> × Σ<sub>j∈A</sub> <span class="math-var">w</span><sub>j</sub> · log <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">y</span><sub>j</sub> | <span class="math-var">y</span><sub>&lt;j</sub>)<br>
  <span class="math-comment">— where A = assistant token positions, C ⊆ A = control token positions</span><br>
  <br>
  <span class="math-var">w</span><sub>silence-first</sub> = <span style="color:var(--green)">1.0</span> &nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="math-comment">first silence in a run</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">w</span><sub>silence-cont</sub> = <span style="color:var(--cyan)">0.4</span> &nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="math-comment">continued silence — down-weighted</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">w</span><sub>response</sub> = <span style="color:var(--purple)">1.5</span> &nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="math-comment">response onset — up-weighted</span>
</div>

<p>Continued silences contribute only 0.4× the gradient signal, while response onsets contribute 1.5×. The model learns to <em>wait</em> without becoming addicted to silence, and to <em>speak</em> without being trigger-happy.</p>
<p>After supervised training, a reinforcement learning stage with GRPO refines the policy against stream-level rewards — crediting correct responses in the right timing window, rewarding appropriate silence, penalizing false alarms and mistimed responses.</p>
</section>

